> > > For a Valkyrie the strategy can be simple - Advance dagger and
> > > long sword to Expert as soon as possible and silver saber and
> > > war hammer to Basic or Skilled as soon as possible ignoring other
> > > weapon classes if needed.

Arguably any character class should advance dagger early in the game.
Not as fast as the primary wielded weapon but as soon as feasible.
Because of the price of skill slots it can make sense to advance
primary wielded weapon to Basic, dagger to Basic, primary to Skilled,
dagger to Skilled, primary to Expert, something else to Basic, yet
another something else to Basic, dagger to Expert, then work on the
others.  Sort of a compromise form of alternation.  Since the primary
weapon tends to change as the game progresses yet use of daggers
does not, this sort of alternation works great.

> > Why bother to advance dagger at all? I usually pull long-sword and
> > war-hammer up to Expert, and saber as high as is feasible.
>
> Because you can quiver a stack of 10 daggers and kill things like nymphs 
> and mindflayers before they get close to you. You'll have a hard time doing 
> that by throwing sabers or long-swords (A Val could possibly do it w/ war-
> hammer).

Even better skill level with dagger gives increased rate of fire.
Being able to throw 1-3 highly enchanted elven dagger per turn makes
an advancing nymph a good source of teleportitis not a good source
of danger.  Throwing several daggers than moving a space back can be
used to keep of flayers away until dead.

